    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im having a bit of an issue with some walnut joinery. im cutting dovetails for a cabinet set and the fibers at the tips of the pins keep splintering or crushing instead of a clean cut. i've already sharpened my chisels but it's still happening. any idea what i might be doing wrong with the grain or the tools?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Walnut joinery can be a bit of a challenge, especially when it comes to those pesky fibers. You're not alone in this struggle, by the way - I've seen many a skilled woodworker wrestle with the same issue. It's a bit like trying to tame a mischievous band of goblins, isn't it? You think you've got the tools and the technique down, but those sneaky fibers just won't cooperate.\n\nNow, let's get down to business. One thing to consider is the grain direction. Are you cutting with the grain, or against it? If you're cutting against the grain, you're essentially trying to cut through a forest of tiny, splinter-prone trees - it's no wonder the fibers are splintering! Try to cut with the grain, and see if that makes a difference. And don't forget to keep your chisels sharp - a dull chisel is like a rusty old sword, only good for chopping at the air.\n\nAnother thing to consider is the angle of your cuts. Are you making smooth, even cuts, or are you applying too much pressure and causing the fibers to split? It's a bit like trying to navigate a treacherous mountain",
      "source": "target",
