    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, what do you mean by \"mischievous band of goblins\"? I'm just trying to figure out my chisel angle and grain management. Can we stay on the technical side? I'm more concerned about the moisture content of the walnut and whether the bevel angle on my chisels is too steep for these fine pins.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy, didn't I? Sorry about that. Let's get back to the technical side of things.\n\nMoisture content is a great point to consider. If the walnut is too dry, the fibers can be more brittle and prone to splintering. On the other hand, if it's too wet, the wood can be soft and difficult to cut. You want to aim for a moisture content that's around 8-10% for walnut. That way, the fibers will be stable and easier to work with.\n\nAs for the bevel angle on your chisels, a steeper angle can indeed cause the fibers to splinter. Try reducing the bevel angle to around 20-25 degrees, and see if that makes a difference. This will help you maintain a cleaner cut and reduce the likelihood of splintering.\n\nAnd don't forget to use a sharp, well-maintained chisel. A dull chisel can cause the fibers to split, even if you're cutting with the grain. It's a bit like trying to cut through a piece of armor with a rusty sword - it just won't work as well.\n\nBy the way, have you ever tried",
      "source": "target",
