    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, I was going to mention that a sharp chisel is essential for making clean cuts. A dull chisel can cause the fibers to splinter, and it's also more likely to bind and tear the wood. It's a bit like trying to navigate a treacherous swamp with a rusty old oar - it just won't work as well.\n\nAnd speaking of tool maintenance, have you ever thought about using a chisel hone to keep your chisels sharp? It's a simple tool that helps to realign the teeth and keep them sharp. It's a bit like giving your chisels a little \"grooming\" session, just like a goblin might groom its own mischievous locks.\n\nBy the way, I've heard that some woodworkers swear by using a \"push cut\" technique when making dovetail pins. Instead of cutting from the center of the pin outwards, you cut from the edge of the pin inwards. This can help to reduce the amount of pressure on the fibers and make the cut cleaner. It's a bit like using a more subtle approach when navigating a goblin's trap - you need to be careful and precise to avoid getting caught.",
